This fixes the issue reported in this comment: https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/pull/14851#issuecomment-3863896385\r\n\r\n```py\r\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\r\n\r\n\r\ndef s_start():\r\n    print(\"SYNC START\", flush=True)\r\n\r\n\r\napp = FastAPI(on_startup=[s_start])\r\n\r\n\r\n@app.get(\"/health\")\r\nasync def health():\r\n    return {\"ok\": True}\r\n```\r\n\r\nStartup event is not called because `APIRouter.on_startup` is set in `APIRouter.__init__` before calling `super().__init__` and  is overridden by `router.__init__` (https://github.com/Kludex/starlette/blob/e5b8a5d200504a2608bed11fe6f66b1d20b9ae2c/starlette/routing.py#L597-L598)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll localize with the graph tools first, starting from the symbols named in the issue.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"search_similar_code\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"__init__\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"__init__\",\n \"matches\": [\n  {\n   \"file\": \"fastapi/routing.py\",\n   \"line\": 163,\n   \"kind\": \"definition\"\n  }\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "__init__ is defined in fastapi/routing.py. Checking its callers and callees before reading any file.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_neighbors\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"__init__\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14873", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "cd31576d5704b6aef1bdadc43770238cd88aad2b", "files": ["fastapi/routing.py"], "symbols": ["__init__", "lifespan"], "n_hunks": 2, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_router_events.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 12, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 1, "tool": "get_code_neighbors", "est_tokens": 1238, "split_from": "fastapi_14873"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\u2728 Add support for dependencies with scopes, support `scope=\"request\"` for dependencies with `yield` that exit before the response is sent\n\n\u2728 Add support for dependencies with scopes, support `scope=\"request\"` for dependencies with `yield` that exit before the response is sent\r\n\r\nThis adds support for\r\n\r\n* `Depends(func, scope=\"request\")`, the default.\r\n* `Depends(func, scope=\"function\")`, early exit, after the function, but before sending the response back.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI'm considering an alternative name for the `scope` parameter of `mode`, to avoid any confusion with OAuth scopes, e.g. `Security(scopes=[\"blah\"])`, which is a completely different idea.\r\n\r\nOr a potential future `Depends(oauth_scopes=[\"blah\"])` to replace `Security(scopes=[\"blah\"])`.\r\n\r\nThat's the only reason to consider `mode` over `scope`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI'm also considering the values, currently they are:\r\n\r\n* `\"function\"`: start before the *path operation function*, exit after the function returns but **before** the response is sent.\r\n* `\"request\"`: start before the *path operation function* (same as above), exit **after** the response is sent back.\r\n\r\nI'm considering an alternative name for `\"request\"` of `\"response\"`, with the same behavior.\r\n\r\nThe rationale for using `\"request\"` as the value is that the dependency runs around (start and end) of the *request* cycle.\r\n\r\nThe rationale for using `\"response\"` as the value is that the main difference with `\"function\"` is when the exit code is run, in `\"function\"` it is run *after* the function is done, in this second form (`\"request\"` or `\"response\"`) it is run *after* the *response* is sent back.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI'm using this interface design because I also plan on (potentially, possibly) add a new `scope=\"lifespan\"` that would allow using dependencies (or equivalent) functions that are run only once per app, and closed when the app ends. This way the same functionality for dependencies could be used for the app lifespan (ASGI `startup` and `shutdown` events). It should be more intuitive for users to work with. But these are future plans.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2025-10-03 Edit\r\n\r\nAfter asking in several places, I concluded to keep the parameter name as `scope`.\r\n\r\nI'll also keep the current values of `\"function\"`, similar to pytest, and `\"request\"`, to avoid confusing users who could think that the dependency will start running when the response is being sent, but it starts when the request starts."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/exceptions.py (hunk at line 147).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/exceptions.py\", \"old_text\": \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\nclass ValidationException(Exception):\\n    def __init__(self, errors: Sequence[Any]) -> None:\\n        self._errors = errors\", \"new_text\": \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\nclass DependencyScopeError(FastAPIError):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    A dependency declared that it depends on another dependency with an invalid\\n    (narrower) scope.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\nclass ValidationException(Exception):\\n    def __init__(self, errors: Sequence[Any]) -> None:\\n        self._errors = errors\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14262", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "425a4c5bb1d1a8e00007e82e5188fc4d30585767", "files": ["docs_src/dependencies/tutorial008e.py", "docs_src/dependencies/tutorial008e_an.py", "docs_src/dependencies/tutorial008e_an_py39.py", "fastapi/dependencies/models.py", "fastapi/dependencies/utils.py", "fastapi/exceptions.py", "fastapi/param_functions.py", "fastapi/params.py", "fastapi/routing.py", "fastapi/types.py"], "symbols": ["get_username", "get_user_me", "Dependant", "cache_key"], "n_hunks": 29, "n_files": 10, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 3, "test_target": ["tests/test_dependency_yield_scope.py", "tests/test_dependency_yield_scope_websockets.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "patch_context", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are patch_context.", "turns": 41, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 26, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1714, "split_from": "fastapi_14262"}}
