ines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\ud83d\udc1b Fix support for functools wraps and partial combined, for async and regular functions and classes in path operations and dependencies\n\n\ud83d\udc1b Fix support for functools wraps and partial combined, for async and regular functions and classes in path operations and dependencies\r\n\r\nThis also uses and extends the tests created by @YuriiMotov :raised_hands: \r\n\r\nShould fix / related to: https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/issues/14444\r\n\r\nThe new tests simulate the multiple combinations of how decorators could affect dependencies and path operation functions (endpoints).\r\n\r\nThe logic to analyze if a function is an async, generator, async generator,  callable instance, etc. now also checks and unwraps and extracts partials in all those levels.\r\n\r\nThe main case is when the callable is an _async_ something (function, generator, etc) because then it needs to be awaited.\r\n\r\nBut the original function could be async or not, the wrapper could be async or not. if either is async, then it has to be awaited, no matter if the other is not async. So, analyzing only the last point in the chain is not enough, it's necessary to analyze the function but also the wrapper.\r\n\r\nThis PR implements and tests all that, all those combinations."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll localize with the graph tools first, starting from the symbols named in the issue.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"search_similar_code\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"_unwrapped_call\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"_unwrapped_call\",\n \"matches\": [\n  {\n   \"file\": \"fastapi/dependencies/models.py\",\n   \"line\": 79,\n   \"kind\": \"definition\"\n  }\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "_unwrapped_call is defined in fastapi/dependencies/models.py. Checking its callers and callees before reading any file.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_neighbors\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"_unwrapped_call\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"_unwrapped_call\",\n \"callers\": [\n  \"_impartial\",\n  \"SecurityRequirement\",\n  \"_uses_scopes\"\n ],\n \"callees\": [\n  \"_impartial\",\n  \"SecurityRequirement\",\n  \"_uses_scopes\"\n ],\n \"note\": \"neighbors derived from symbols co-occurring in the patch\"\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Getting the surrounding call graph so I read only the files that matter.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_subgraph\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"_unwrapped_call\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14448", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "c57ac7bdf3613798c94caceff562a41fa16d4a2d", "files": ["fastapi/dependencies/models.py", "fastapi/dependencies/utils.py"], "symbols": ["_unwrapped_call", "_impartial", "SecurityRequirement", "_uses_scopes"], "n_hunks": 3, "n_files": 2, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_dependency_wrapped.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 14, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 2, "tool": "get_code_subgraph", "est_tokens": 1496, "split_from": "fastapi_14448"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Test on urllib3 1.26.x\n\nFirst step to address #6734. We'll enable testing on urllib3 1.26.x which was lost when we moved the pin to support 2.x. These will fail for now until the underlying issue is addressed, but this is to ensure we're catching any future breakages."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll localize with the graph tools first, starting from the symbols named in the issue.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"search_similar_code\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"super_len\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"super_len\",\n \"matches\": [\n  {\n   \"file\": \"src/requests/utils.py\",\n   \"line\": 135,\n   \"kind\": \"definition\"\n  }\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "super_len is defined in src/requests/utils.py. Checking its callers and callees before reading any file.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_neighbors\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"super_len\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "requests_6757", "repo": "psf/requests", "base_commit": "0e322af87745eff34caffe4df68456ebc20d9068", "files": ["src/requests/compat.py", "src/requests/utils.py"], "symbols": ["super_len", "split", "except"], "n_hunks": 3, "n_files": 2, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_requests.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 14, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 1, "tool": "get_code_neighbors", "est_tokens": 1139, "split_from": "requests_6757"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\ud83d\udc1b Strip whitespaces from `Authorization` header credentials\n\n## Description\r\n\r\nFixes whitespace handling in authorization header parameter extraction to comply with [RFC 6750](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6750#section-2.1).\r\n\r\n## Problem\r\n\r\nThe current implementation preserves leading/trailing whitespace in the extracted credential parameter. While RFC 6750 allows one or more spaces (`1*SP`) between \"Bearer\" and the token, the token definition (`b64token`) doesn't include whitespace characters, meaning spaces should be treated as separators only.\r\n\r\nThis causes the extracted credentials to include extraneous whitespace, which can lead to authentication failures.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nAdded `.strip()` to the parameter extraction to remove leading and trailing whitespace:\r\n```python\r\nreturn scheme, param.strip()\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Reproduction\r\n\r\n### Before the fix:\r\n```python\r\nfrom typing import Annotated\r\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI\r\nfrom fastapi.security import HTTPAuthorizationCredentials, HTTPBearer\r\n\r\napp = FastAPI()\r\nsecurity = HTTPBearer()\r\n\r\n@app.get(\"/users/me\")\r\ndef read_current_user(\r\n    credentials: Annotated[HTTPAuthorizationCredentials, Depends(security)]\r\n):\r\n    return {\"scheme\": credentials.scheme, \"credentials\": credentials.credentials}\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Test case 1: Single space (correct)**\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -X 'GET' \\\r\n  'http://127.0.0.1:8000/users/me' \\\r\n  -H 'Authorization: Bearer xxx'\r\n```\r\nResponse: `{\"scheme\":\"Bearer\",\"credentials\":\"xxx\"}` \u2713\r\n\r\n**Test case 2: Multiple spaces**\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -X 'GET' \\\r\n  'http://127.0.0.1:8000/users/me' \\\r\n  -H 'Authorization: Bearer   xxx'\r\n```\r\nResponse: `{\"scheme\":\"Bearer\",\"credentials\":\"  xxx\"}` \u2717\r\n\r\n**Test case 3: Trailing whitespace**\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -X 'GET' \\\r\n  'http://127.0.0.1:8000/users/me' \\\r\n  -H 'Authorization: Bearer xxx '\r\n```\r\nResponse: `{\"scheme\":\"Bearer\",\"credentials\":\"xxx \"}` \u2717\r\n\r\n### After the fix:\r\n\r\nAll three test cases now return: `{\"scheme\":\"Bearer\",\"credentials\":\"xxx\"}` \u2713\r\n\r\n## Changes\r\n\r\n- Modified `get_authorization_scheme_param()` in `fastapi/security/utils.py` to strip whitespace from the parameter\r\n- Added test cases to verify the fix handles multiple spaces and trailing whitespace correctly\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [x] Tests added to cover the bug fix\r\n- [x] Existing tests pass"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Running the repro to confirm the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python /tmp/repro_fastapi_14786.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "FAIL: \ud83d\udc1b Strip whitespaces from `Authorization` header credentials ## Description Fixes whitespace handling in authorization header parameter extraction to comply with [RFC 6750](https://datatracker.ietf.org..."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/security/utils.py (hunk at line 7).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/security/utils.py\", \"old_text\": \"    if not authorization_header_value:\\n        return \\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"\\n    scheme, _, param = authorization_header_value.partition(\\\" \\\")\\n    return scheme, param\", \"new_text\": \"    if not authorization_header_value:\\n        return \\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"\\n    scheme, _, param = authorization_header_value.partition(\\\" \\\")\\n    return scheme, param.strip()\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Re-running the repro after the edit to check the fix.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python /tmp/repro_fastapi_14786.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "PASS: repro script now succeeds (no failure output after the fix)."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Repro passes. Now the narrowest relevant tests.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python -m pytest tests/test_security_http_base.py tests/test_security_oauth2_authorization_code_bearer.py -q\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "PASS: pytest tests/test_security_http_base.py tests/test_security_oauth2_authorization_code_bearer.py \u2014 all collected tests passed after the fix."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Tests pass. Confirming the diff touches only library code before submitting.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_status\", \"arguments\": {}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14786", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "eacbce24c9d299c6a28110d9fc8ac50f53cddb08", "files": ["fastapi/security/utils.py"], "symbols": ["get_authorization_scheme_param", "strip"], "n_hunks": 1, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_security_http_base.py", "tests/test_security_oauth2_authorization_code_bearer.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 11, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 9, "tool": "get_status", "est_tokens": 1939, "split_from": "fastapi_14786"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Update progress.py\n\nDon't output extraneous blank line when progress display is disabled\r\n\r\n\r\n<!--\r\nPlease note that Rich isn't accepting any new features at this point.\r\n\r\nIf a feature can be implemented without modifying the core library, then\r\nthey should be released as a third-party module. I can accept updates to the\r\ncore library that make it easier to extend (think hooks).\r\n\r\nBugfixes are always welcome of course.\r\n\r\nSometimes it is not clear what is a feature and what is a bug fix.\r\nIf there is any doubt, please open a discussion first.\r\n\r\n-->\r\n\r\n<!--\r\n*Are you contributing typo fixes?*\r\n\r\nIf your PR solely consists of typos, at least one must be in the docs to warrant an addition to CONTRIBUTORS.md\r\n-->\r\n\r\n## Type of changes\r\n\r\n- [x] Bug fix\r\n- [ ] New feature\r\n- [ ] Documentation / docstrings\r\n- [ ] Tests\r\n- [ ] Other\r\n\r\n## AI?\r\n\r\n- [ ] AI was used to generate this PR\r\n\r\nAI generated PRs may be accepted, but only if @willmcgugan has responded on an issue or discussion.\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [x] I've run the latest [black](https://github.com/psf/black) with default args on new code.\r\n- [x] I've updated CHANGELOG.md and CONTRIBUTORS.md where appropriate (see note about typos above).\r\n- [x] I've added tests for new code.\r\n- [x] I accept that @willmcgugan may be pedantic in the code review.\r\n\r\n## Description\r\n\r\nWhen creating a `Progress` with `disabled=True`, it still outputs a blank line on non interactive consoles. This PR adds an `if not self.disable` to the `stop` function, symmetrically to the `start` function.\r\n\r\n**Important:** Link to an issue or discussion regarding these changes. \r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/Textualize/rich/issues/110\r\n"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Before editing anything, I'll write a small repro script under /tmp that triggers the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"write_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"/tmp/repro_rich_3905.py\", \"content\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Repro for rich_3905 (programmatic replay; generic script).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\"/workspace\\\")\\n\\nfrom rich.progress import start  # noqa: F401\\n\\n# Reported failure (from the issue):\\n# Update progress.py Don't output extraneous blank line when progress display is disabled <!-- Please note that Rich isn't accepting any new features at this point. If a feature can be implemented witho...\\n# A faithful repro needs the exact failing call from the issue, so\\n# this script confirms the module imports and the symbol exists;\\n# the failure itself is triggered from the issue's example.\\nprint(\\\"repro ready:\\\", start)\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "File written."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Running the repro to confirm the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python /tmp/repro_rich_3905.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_3905", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "96dd4b60e3a7c647c43a85ad375afa690b3bb85b", "files": ["rich/progress.py"], "symbols": ["start", "stop", "__enter__"], "n_hunks": 1, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_progress.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 11, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 5, "tool": "run_command", "est_tokens": 1621, "split_from": "rich_3905"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Fix double-width characters disappearing when wrapping\n\n## Type of changes\r\n\r\n- [x] Bug fix\r\n- [ ] New feature\r\n- [ ] Documentation / docstrings\r\n- [ ] Tests\r\n- [ ] Other\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [x] I've run the latest [black](https://github.com/psf/black) with default args on new code.\r\n- [x] I've updated CHANGELOG.md and CONTRIBUTORS.md where appropriate.\r\n- [x] I've added tests for new code.\r\n- [x] I accept that @willmcgugan may be pedantic in the code review.\r\n\r\n## Description\r\n\r\nUpdate wrapping logic to fix issues with CJK charcters disappearing when the \"fold\" location sat *within* a double-width character. Ensure we retain browser logic of: \r\n\r\n\r\n> if there is no space on the current line, move to a new line, and if theres not enough space on the entire new line, fold the text over multiple lines at appropriate locations.\r\n\r\nAdds some additional tests and docstrings, documentation etc.\r\n\r\nFixes #3176 \r\n\r\nThe wrapping process is overall still quite simple and doesn't match the browser in many cases. For example, wrapping does not consider punctuation (lines can begin with punctuation), and whitespace is handled differently (but practically speaking it seems sensible)."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll localize with the graph tools first, starting from the symbols named in the issue.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"search_similar_code\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"words\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"words\",\n \"matches\": [\n  {\n   \"file\": \"rich/_wrap.py\",\n   \"line\": 10,\n   \"kind\": \"definition\"\n  }\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "words is defined in rich/_wrap.py. Checking its callers and callees before reading any file.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_neighbors\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"words\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"words\",\n \"callers\": [\n  \"divide_line\",\n  \"set_cell_size\",\n  \"chop_cells\"\n ],\n \"callees\": [\n  \"divide_line\",\n  \"set_cell_size\",\n  \"chop_cells\"\n ],\n \"note\": \"neighbors derived from symbols co-occurring in the patch\"\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Getting the surrounding call graph so I read only the files that matter.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_subgraph\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"words\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"nodes\": [\n  {\n   \"symbol\": \"words\",\n   \"file\": \"rich/_wrap.py\",\n   \"line\": 10\n  },\n  {\n   \"symbol\": \"divide_line\",\n   \"file\": \"rich/_wrap.py\",\n   \"line\": 20\n  },\n  {\n   \"symbol\": \"set_cell_size\",\n   \"file\": \"rich/cells.py\",\n   \"line\": 87\n  },\n  {\n   \"symbol\": \"chop_cells\",\n   \"file\": \"rich/cells.py\",\n   \"line\": 124\n  }\n ],\n \"edges\": [\n  [\n   \"words\",\n   \"divide_line\"\n  ],\n  [\n   \"words\",\n   \"set_cell_size\"\n  ],\n  [\n   \"words\",\n   \"chop_cells\"\n  ]\n ],\n \"files\": [\n  \"rich/_wrap.py\",\n  \"rich/cells.py\"\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Reading rich/_wrap.py around the suspect symbol.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"read_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/_wrap.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_3180", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "e76f3c3ea78481342e21a7b3328075462ab69c2b", "files": ["rich/_wrap.py", "rich/cells.py"], "symbols": ["words", "divide_line", "set_cell_size", "chop_cells"], "n_hunks": 4, "n_files": 2, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_cells.py", "tests/test_text.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 15, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 3, "tool": "read_file", "est_tokens": 1623, "split_from": "rich_3180"}}
