{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Handle graphemes\n\nRich has never handled multi-codepoint emoji, because terminals were highly inconsistent about how they were rendered. And there was no way to know in advance how wide a given collection of codepoints was going to be. \r\n\r\nThis is technically still the case. However modern terminals are more consistent now, and they tend to follow unicode rules. Which means that Rich can also follow those rules and get it right more of the time.\r\n\r\nThis PR uses the data from https://github.com/jquast/wcwidth to calculate widths in the same way. I didn't use wcwidth directly, as Rich has more complex requirements. Calculating the width of a string is not enough, if you want to do wrapping, cropping etc.\r\n\r\nThis PR adds the capability to split strings in to sequences that produce a single glyph in the terminal. And that is used in a few low-level functions.\r\n\r\nThe data is stored in a way that keeps startup time low, and makes width calculations more optimal than wcwidth.\r\n\r\nThis iteration should hopefully offer similar performance to the earlier versions. In the nearish future there will be a Rust library to speed up some of the lower level operations.\r\n\r\nFixes https://github.com/Textualize/rich/issues/3897"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/cells.py (hunk at line 21).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/cells.py\", \"old_text\": \"    (0x02800, 0x028FF),  # Braille\\n]\\n\\n# A set of characters that are a single cell wide\\n_SINGLE_CELLS = frozenset(\\n    [\\n        character\", \"new_text\": \"    (0x02800, 0x028FF),  # Braille\\n]\\n\\n# A frozen set of characters that are a single cell wide\\n_SINGLE_CELLS = frozenset(\\n    [\\n        character\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/cells.py (hunk at line 307).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/cells.py\", \"old_text\": \"        A list of strings such that each string in the list has cell width\\n        less than or equal to the available width.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    _get_character_cell_size = get_character_cell_size\\n    lines: list[list[str]] = [[]]\\n\\n    append_new_line = lines.append\\n    append_to_last_line = lines[-1].append\\n\\n    total_width = 0\\n\\n    for character in text:\\n        cell_width = _get_character_cell_size(character)\\n        char_doesnt_fit = total_width + cell_width > width\\n\\n        if char_doesnt_fit:\\n            append_new_line([character])\\n            append_to_last_line = lines[-1].append\\n            total_width = cell_width\\n        else:\\n            append_to_last_line(character)\\n            total_width += cell_width\\n\\n    return [\\\"\\\".join(line) for line in lines]\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":  # pragma: no cover\\n    print(get_character_cell_size(\\\"\\ud83d\\ude3d\\\"))\\n    for line in chop_cells(\\\"\\\"\\\"\\u8fd9\\u662f\\u5bf9\\u4e9a\\u6d32\\u8bed\\u8a00\\u652f\\u6301\\u7684\\u6d4b\\u8bd5\\u3002\\u9762\\u5bf9\\u6a21\\u68f1\\u4e24\\u53ef\\u7684\\u60f3\\u6cd5\\uff0c\\u62d2\\u7edd\\u731c\\u6d4b\\u7684\\u8bf1\\u60d1\\u3002\\\"\\\"\\\", 8):\\n        print(line)\\n    for n in range(80, 1, -1):\\n        print(set_cell_size(\\\"\\\"\\\"\\u8fd9\\u662f\\u5bf9\\u4e9a\\u6d32\\u8bed\\u8a00\\u652f\\u6301\\u7684\\u6d4b\\u8bd5\\u3002\\u9762\\u5bf9\\u6a21\\u68f1\\u4e24\\u53ef\\u7684\\u60f3\\u6cd5\\uff0c\\u62d2\\u7edd\\u731c\\u6d4b\\u7684\\u8bf1\\u60d1\\u3002\\\"\\\"\\\", n) + \\\"|\\\")\\n        print(\\\"x\\\" * n)\", \"new_text\": \"        A list of strings such that each string in the list has cell width\\n        less than or equal to the available width.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if _is_single_cell_widths(text):\\n        return [text[index : index + width] for index in range(0, len(text), width)]\\n    spans, _ = split_graphemes(text, unicode_version)\\n    line_size = 0  # Size of line in cells\\n    lines: list[str] = []\\n    line_offset = 0  # Offset (in codepoints) of start of line\\n    for start, end, cell_size in spans:\\n        if line_size + cell_size > width:\\n            lines.append(text[line_offset:start])\\n            line_offset = start\\n            line_size = 0\\n        line_size += cell_size\\n    if line_size:\\n        lines.append(text[line_offset:])\\n\\n    return lines\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_3930", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "53757bc234cf18977cade41a5b64f3abaccb0b85", "files": ["rich/__main__.py", "rich/_cell_widths.py", "rich/_unicode_data/__init__.py", "rich/_unicode_data/_versions.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode10-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode11-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode12-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode12-1-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode13-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode14-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode15-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode15-1-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode16-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode17-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode4-1-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode5-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode5-1-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode5-2-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode6-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode6-1-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode6-2-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode6-3-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode7-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode8-0-0.py", "rich/_unicode_data/unicode9-0-0.py", "rich/cells.py"], "symbols": ["iter_last", "_parse_version", "load", "CellTable"], "n_hunks": 29, "n_files": 26, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 23, "test_target": ["tests/test_cells.py", "tests/test_text.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "patch_context", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are patch_context.", "turns": 41, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 36, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1967, "split_from": "rich_3930"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\ud83d\udc1b Fix evaluating stringified annotations in Python 3.10\n\nI've bumped into the exact same issue as https://github.com/tiangolo/typer/discussions/598 so I solved it like https://github.com/tiangolo/typer/pull/721  :smile: \r\n\r\nI'll try to add tests soon but if you can do it faster, please feel free !!"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Reading fastapi/dependencies/utils.py around the suspect symbol.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"read_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/dependencies/utils.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "fastapi/dependencies/utils.py (lines 1-150 of 1037):\n1| import dataclasses\n2| import inspect\n3| from contextlib import AsyncExitStack, contextmanager\n4| from copy import copy, deepcopy\n5| from dataclasses import dataclass\n6| from typing import (\n7|     Any,\n8|     Callable,\n9|     Coroutine,\n10|     Dict,\n11|     ForwardRef,\n12|     List,\n13|     Mapping,\n14|     Optional,\n15|     Sequence,\n16|     Tuple,\n17|     Type,\n18|     Union,\n19|     cast,\n20| )\n21| \n22| import anyio\n23| from fastapi import params\n24| from fastapi._compat import (\n25|     PYDANTIC_V2,\n26|     ModelField,\n27|     RequiredParam,\n28|     Undefined,\n29|     _is_error_wrapper,\n30|     _is_model_class,\n31|     copy_field_info,\n32|     create_body_model,\n33|     evaluate_forwardref,\n34|     field_annotation_is_scalar,\n35|     get_annotation_from_field_info,\n36|     get_cached_model_fields,\n37|     get_missing_field_error,\n38|     is_bytes_field,\n39|     is_bytes_sequence_field,\n40|     is_scalar_field,\n41|     is_scalar_sequence_field,\n42|     is_sequence_field,\n43|     is_uploadfile_or_nonable_uploadfile_annotation,\n44|     is_uploadfile_sequence_annotation,\n45|     lenient_issubclass,\n46|     may_v1,\n47|     sequence_types,\n48|     serialize_sequence_value,\n49|     value_is_sequence,\n50| )\n51| from fastapi._compat.shared import annotation_is_pydantic_v1\n52| from fastapi.background import BackgroundTasks\n53| from fastapi.concurrency import (\n54|     asynccontex