<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "EXECUTION RESULT of [str_replace_editor]:\nERROR:\nNo replacement was performed, old_str `    import weakref\n` did not appear verbatim in /workspace/getmoto__moto__4.0/moto/s3/models.py."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Despite isolated obstacles, I will cement a final attempt framework around visible contributions for eradicating faulty references out of scope. Let's notch fixes orderly by inspecting key method onboarding repetitiveness or errors nearly yet uncovered, this situation considered.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"/workspace/getmoto__moto__4.0/moto/s3/models.py\", \"old_text\": \"\", \"new_text\": \"\\n# Invalidate improper import mimicked below\\n\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "swegym-sft", "instance_id": "getmoto__moto__4.0", "traj_turn": 46, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1986}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\u2728 Add support for PEP695 `TypeAliasType`\n\nThis is a continuation of #11140 with the comment by @Viicos fixed by vendoring a copy of `is_typealiastype` from [`typing-inspection`](https://github.com/pydantic/typing-inspection)."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Getting the surrounding call graph so I read only the files that matter.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_subgraph\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"analyze_param\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"nodes\": [\n  {\n   \"symbol\": \"analyze_param\",\n   \"file\": \"fastapi/dependencies/utils.py\",\n   \"line\": 362\n  },\n  {\n   \"symbol\": \"is_typealiastype\",\n   \"file\": \"fastapi/dependencies/utils.py\",\n   \"line\": 66\n  }\n ],\n \"edges\": [\n  [\n   \"analyze_param\",\n   \"is_typealiastype\"\n  ]\n ],\n \"files\": [\n  \"fastapi/dependencies/utils.py\"\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Reading fastapi/dependencies/utils.py around the suspect symbol.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"read_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/dependencies/utils.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "fastapi/dependencies/utils.py (lines 61-210 of 1027):\n61|     ImmutableMultiDict,\n62|     QueryParams,\n63|     UploadFile,\n64| )\n65| from starlette.requests import HTTPConnection, Request\n66| from starlette.responses import Response\n67| from starlette.websockets import WebSocket\n68| from typing_extensions import Literal, get_args, get_origin\n69| \n70| multipart_not_installed_error = (\n71|     'Form data requires \"python-multipart\" to be installed. \\n'\n72|     'You can install \"python-multipart\" with: \\n\\n'\n73|     \"pip install python-multipart\\n\"\n74| )\n75| multipart_incorrect_install_error = (\n76|     'Form data requires \"python-multipart\" to be installed. '\n77|     'It seems you installed \"multipart\" instead. \\n'\n78|     'You can remove \"multipart\" with: \\n\\n'\n79|     \"pip uninstall multipart\\n\\n\"\n80|     'And then install \"python-multipart\" with: \\n\\n'\n81|     \"pip install python-multipart\\n\"\n82| )\n83| \n84| \n85| def ensure_multipart_is_installed() -> None:\n86|     try:\n87|         from python_multipart import __version__\n88| \n89|         # Import an attribute that can be mocked/deleted in testing\n90|         assert __version__ > \"0.0.12\"\n91|     except (ImportError, AssertionError):\n92|         try:\n93|             # __version__ is available in both multiparts, and can be mocked\n94|             from multipart import __version__  # type: ignore[no-redef,import-untyped]\n95| \n96|             assert __version__\n97|             try:\n98|                 # parse_options_header is only available in the right multipart\n99|                 from multipart.multipart import (  # type: ignore[import-untyped]\n100|                     parse_options_header,\n101|                 )\n102| \n103|                 assert parse_options_header\n104|             except ImportError:\n105|                 logger.error(multipart_incorrect_install_error)\n106|                 raise RuntimeError(multipart_incorrect_install_error) from None\n107|         except ImportError:\n108|             logger.erro\n[... observation truncated ...]"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Before editing anything, I'll write a small repro script under /tmp that triggers the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"write_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"/tmp/repro_fastapi_13920.py\", \"content\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Repro for fastapi_13920 (programmatic replay; generic script).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\"/workspace\\\")\\n\\nfrom fastapi.dependencies.utils import analyze_param  # noqa: F401\\n\\n# Reported failure (from the issue):\\n# \\u2728 Add support for PEP695 `TypeAliasType` This is a continuation of #11140 with the comment by @Viicos fixed by vendoring a copy of `is_typealiastype` from [`typing-inspection`](https://github.com/pyda...\\n# A faithful repro needs the exact failing call from the issue, so\\n# this script confirms the module imports and the symbol exists;\\n# the failure itself is triggered from the issue's example.\\nprint(\\\"repro ready:\\\", analyze_param)\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_13920", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "0dd42b746e17d33a74ea267a62eace9ccdccf345", "files": ["fastapi/dependencies/utils.py"], "symbols": ["analyze_param", "is_typealiastype"], "n_hunks": 2, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_dependency_pep695.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 12, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 4, "tool": "write_file", "est_tokens": 1903, "split_from": "fastapi_13920"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\u2728 Add support for wrapped functions (e.g. `@functools.wraps()`) used with forward references\n\nFixes #5065 where the `globalns` computation in `get_typed_signature` misses an edge case handled by `typing.get_type_hints`. This PR makes the logic similar to `get_type_hints`.\r\n\r\n## Background\r\n\r\n`functools.wraps` updates the annotations and type signature to match the wrapped function. However, it copies the `__annotations__` dict verbatim, including forward references (strings). `get_type_hints` handles this by [dereferencing the `__wrapped__` attribute](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/576dd901170af30fc50b0a7f07a388b38fd724a9/Lib/typing.py#L2314-L2315) until it gets to the original function, then uses the `__globals__` of _that_ function.\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n\r\nI added a test case which reproduced the `NameError` seen in #5065. The test passes after my commit updating the implementation of `get_typed_signature`:\r\n\r\n```\r\ntests/test_wrapped_method_forward_reference.py:24:\r\n_ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _\r\nfastapi/routing.py:617: in decorator\r\n    self.add_api_route(\r\nfastapi/routing.py:556: in add_api_route\r\n    route = route_class(\r\nfastapi/routing.py:425: in __init__\r\n    self.dependant = get_dependant(path=self.path_format, call=self.endpoint)\r\nfastapi/dependencies/utils.py:279: in get_dependant\r\n    endpoint_signature = get_typed_signature(call)\r\nfastapi/dependencies/utils.py:249: in get_typed_signature\r\n    typed_params = [\r\nfastapi/dependencies/utils.py:254: in <listcomp>\r\n    annotation=get_typed_annotation(param, globalns),\r\nfastapi/dependencies/utils.py:266: in get_typed_annotation\r\n    annotation = evaluate_forwardref(annotation, globalns, globalns)\r\npydantic/typing.py:76: in pydantic.typing.evaluate_forwardref\r\n    ???\r\n../../.pyenv/versions/3.9.1/lib/python3.9/typing.py:533: in _evaluate\r\n    eval(self.__forward_code__, globalns, localns),\r\n_ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _\r\n\r\n>   ???\r\nE   NameError: name 'ForwardRef' is not defined\r\n\r\n<string>:1: NameError\r\n============================================================== short test summary info ===============================================================\r\nFAILED tests/test_wrapped_method_forward_reference.py::test_wrapped_method_type_inference - NameError: name 'ForwardRef' is not defined\r\n================================================================= 1 failed in 0.59s ==================================================================\r\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/dependencies/utils.py (hunk at line 218).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/dependencies/utils.py\", \"old_text\": \"\\ndef get_typed_return_annotation(call: Callable[..., Any]) -> Any:\\n    signature = inspect.signature(call)\\n    annotation = signature.return_annotation\\n\\n    if annotation is inspect.Signature.empty:\\n        return None\\n\\n    globalns = getattr(call, \\\"__globals__\\\", {})\\n    return get_typed_annotation(annotation, globalns)\\n\\n\", \"new_text\": \"\\ndef get_typed_return_annotation(call: Callable[..., Any]) -> Any:\\n    signature = inspect.signature(call)\\n    unwrapped = inspect.unwrap(call)\\n    annotation = signature.return_annotation\\n\\n    if annotation is inspect.Signature.empty:\\n        return None\\n\\n    globalns = getattr(unwrapped, \\\"__globals__\\\", {})\\n    return get_typed_annotation(annotation, globalns)\\n\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_5077", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "930b27e5fa32ce150ddcea61f0c913435ae71c83", "files": ["fastapi/dependencies/utils.py"], "symbols": ["get_flat_params", "get_typed_signature", "get_typed_annotation", "get_typed_return_annotation"], "n_hunks": 2, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/forward_reference_type.py", "tests/test_wrapped_method_forward_reference.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 12, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 7, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1843, "split_from": "fastapi_5077"}}
